    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **277 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including the new `Cp16Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature by following the established pattern in this codebase for owner attributes assigned at creation (like `membershipNumber` and `membershipTier`). The acceptance test reads the value back via a separate `GET /api/owners/{id}` request, so the count had to be **persisted**, not just returned inline.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New OfficeFloor step** \u2014 `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java`: counts existing owners in the repository whose last name equals the new owner's last name (excluding the same owner by id). Since the new owner isn't saved until a later step, every match is genuinely an \"other\" owner at that moment.\n\n2. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: inserted `assignNamesakeCount` (under `transaction` governance) between `assignMembershipTier` and `save`.\n\n3. **Entity** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added the `namesake_count` column field with getter/setter.\n\n4. **Schema** \u2014 added `namesake_count` (INTEGER/INT/INT(4)) to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n5. **API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added the read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, which regenerates `OwnerDto` and is auto-mapped by MapStruct (both on the create response and the GET response).\n\nThe `count of *other* owners` semantic is verified by the test: the first owner of a surname reports `0`, the second reports `1`.",
